<article class="env-proposition" id="AFC3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/AFC3">Proposition <span data-tag="AFC3">5.4.7.4</span>.</a>compact object!limits of Let $\calC $ be a $\kappa $-accessible $\infty $-category, and let $\tau \gg \kappa $ be an uncountable regular cardinal such that $\calC ^{\kappa }$ is essentially $\tau $-small. Then the full subcategory $\calC ^{\tau } \subseteq \calC $ is stable under all $\kappa $-small limits which exist in $\calC $. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/AFC3" data-tag="AFC3">5.4.7.4</a>.</strong>
       Let $K$ be a $\kappa $-small simplicial set and let $p: K \rightarrow \calC ^{\tau }$ be a diagram which admits a limit $X \in \calC $. We wish to show that $X$ is $\tau $-compact. According to Lemma <a href="/tag/93C2" data-tag="93C2">5.4.7.5</a>, it suffices to prove that the space $F(X)$ is essentially $\tau $-small, where $F: \calC \rightarrow \SSet $ denotes the functor co-represented by a $\kappa $-compact object $C \in \calC $. Since $F$ preserves limits, we note that $F(X)$ is a limit of $F \circ p$. Lemma <a href="/tag/93C2" data-tag="93C2">5.4.7.5</a> implies that the diagram $F \circ p$ takes values in $\SSet ^{\tau } \subseteq \SSet $. We now conclude by applying Lemma <a href="/tag/4F64" data-tag="4F64">5.4.7.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>